WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.274 --> 00:00:02.803
- He's an entrepreneur,
television personality,

00:00:02.803 --> 00:00:05.188
real estate magnet and investor.

00:00:05.188 --> 00:00:09.355
He was number 17 on Forbes'
2011 Celebrity 100 list.

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:12.141
He even considered running

00:00:12.141 --> 00:00:14.608
for President of the
United States of America.

00:00:14.608 --> 00:00:15.643
He's Donald Trump,

00:00:15.643 --> 00:00:19.143
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:20.741 --> 00:00:23.484
- In your book The Art of the Deal,

00:00:23.484 --> 00:00:25.984
you said that money really
isn't that important.

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:28.012
It's just a means of keeping score.

00:00:28.012 --> 00:00:30.673
It's playing the game that excites you.

00:00:30.673 --> 00:00:31.849
- I love the game.

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:34.575
I like the money and
the money is certainly

00:00:34.575 --> 00:00:38.526
a method of keeping score, but
I don't do things for money.

00:00:38.526 --> 00:00:40.959
I do things because I enjoy doing them.

00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:42.821
That really can sometimes translate

00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:44.261
into more money than you would have made

00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:45.869
if you weren't just after the money.

00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:46.786
Never, ever

00:00:48.197 --> 00:00:49.788
give up.

00:00:49.788 --> 00:00:53.209
You can change and you can move around

00:00:53.209 --> 00:00:55.126
but never ever give up.

00:00:57.373 --> 00:01:00.093
I know people that are very smart.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:02.497
They went to the Wharton
School of Finance with me.

00:01:02.497 --> 00:01:05.186
I know other people that aren't as smart

00:01:05.186 --> 00:01:07.833
and those people are the top
people in the industry today

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:10.090
and they top, they just were tougher.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.369
They didn't give up.

00:01:11.369 --> 00:01:13.457
And the smart ones had
everything on their plate.

00:01:13.457 --> 00:01:15.922
They'd always come in with
the A plus on the tests

00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:18.136
and the good boards and everything else.

00:01:18.136 --> 00:01:19.945
But when they ran into problems,

00:01:19.945 --> 00:01:21.781
they did not know how
to solve the problems

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.535
like the guys that weren't as good.

00:01:23.535 --> 00:01:26.180
And it's very seldom that you see

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.763
something other than this happen.

00:01:28.763 --> 00:01:30.596
So never ever give up.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:33.812
A lot of that is all in the details.

00:01:33.812 --> 00:01:35.808
You've got to be into the details

00:01:35.808 --> 00:01:37.445
because if you're not

00:01:37.445 --> 00:01:39.357
they'll come back to haunt you.

00:01:39.357 --> 00:01:42.961
So, often times you know,
life is in the details

00:01:42.961 --> 00:01:44.971
well business is also in the details.

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:46.615
You have to be very careful,

00:01:46.615 --> 00:01:48.153
you have to watch the dotted I's

00:01:48.153 --> 00:01:50.548
because you can really lose tremendously

00:01:50.548 --> 00:01:53.401
if you don't watch those details.

00:01:53.401 --> 00:01:57.098
So we discussed that and
as Robert likes to say

00:01:57.098 --> 00:01:59.625
probably more than I do,
that's the little finger.

00:01:59.625 --> 00:02:01.553
But it's all about the details.

00:02:01.553 --> 00:02:03.550
That's very important.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.717
You want to be totally focused.

00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:10.091
You can't take your eye off the ball.

00:02:10.091 --> 00:02:13.051
When I had troubles in the early 90's,

00:02:13.051 --> 00:02:16.497
a major article came out
and it said very strongly,

00:02:16.497 --> 00:02:19.314
everything he touches turns to gold.

00:02:19.314 --> 00:02:20.973
And I believed it.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:22.697
So I would go out with models that night

00:02:22.697 --> 00:02:24.471
instead of working

00:02:24.471 --> 00:02:25.694
but that wasn't good.

00:02:25.694 --> 00:02:28.186
And I remember, I had
a big lease coming up,

00:02:28.186 --> 00:02:30.402
there was a big show,
and I went to the show.

00:02:30.402 --> 00:02:32.146
I said, don't worry fellas,
you can handle the lease,

00:02:32.146 --> 00:02:32.979
you'll get it done.

00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:34.629
Well I came back, they didn't get it done,

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:36.501
and I would have had it done 100%.

00:02:36.501 --> 00:02:38.219
And then the market crashed in the 90's,

00:02:38.219 --> 00:02:39.759
so I had trouble

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:40.820
but it was sort of an amazing,

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:41.702
I wouldn't want to do it again

00:02:41.702 --> 00:02:44.034
but it was amazing test of yourself.

00:02:44.034 --> 00:02:45.570
Can you handle pressure?

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.074
How are you under pressure?

00:02:47.074 --> 00:02:48.297
Are you smart?

00:02:48.297 --> 00:02:51.176
You know, when everybody's coming at you.

00:02:51.176 --> 00:02:55.004
You know, the worst
employee is a good employee.

00:02:55.004 --> 00:02:56.468
A bad employee is fine

00:02:56.468 --> 00:02:58.962
because you fire that person right away.

00:02:58.962 --> 00:03:00.950
A great employee is phenomenal.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.902
You want to keep them and cherish them.

00:03:02.902 --> 00:03:04.485
They're fantastic.

00:03:04.485 --> 00:03:07.131
But a good employee, you
never sort of fire them.

00:03:07.131 --> 00:03:10.441
But they never leave
you to the next level.

00:03:10.441 --> 00:03:12.128
They just keep their job.

00:03:12.128 --> 00:03:14.548
They're sort of too good to be fired,

00:03:14.548 --> 00:03:16.229
but they're not good enough.

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.111
You're never going to do great.

00:03:18.111 --> 00:03:21.709
So I always say, the worst
employee isn't a bad employee.

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:25.069
It's just a good or average employee.

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:27.159
Gary Player, the great golfer,

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:29.904
he was a little guy, very little,

00:03:29.904 --> 00:03:31.687
but he worked really hard

00:03:31.687 --> 00:03:33.702
and he had a great statement.

00:03:33.702 --> 00:03:38.048
He'd go, the harder I
work, the luckier I get.

00:03:38.048 --> 00:03:39.163
And he wins the US Open.

00:03:39.163 --> 00:03:40.819
You have all these big handsome guys,

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:44.014
these big giant guys, and
they'd hit the ball a mile,

00:03:44.014 --> 00:03:45.407
and you have Gary Player.

00:03:45.407 --> 00:03:46.937
And Gary Player would win the Open.

00:03:46.937 --> 00:03:47.920
He would win the Masters.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.457
He would win this, he'd win that.

00:03:49.457 --> 00:03:51.554
And he said, you know, hey.

00:03:51.554 --> 00:03:53.344
In the US Open he hit a five iron

00:03:53.344 --> 00:03:54.632
to one foot from the hole

00:03:54.632 --> 00:03:55.676
and he ended up getting a birdie

00:03:55.676 --> 00:03:57.586
and wins the US Open right.

00:03:57.586 --> 00:03:58.843
And he came off the green,

00:03:58.843 --> 00:04:00.472
and I know I've heard
this statement before,

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:02.579
but I heard it from him
in a meaningful way.

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:04.965
They said what do you
think Gary, what is it?

00:04:04.965 --> 00:04:07.593
He said, all I know is
I've been working very hard

00:04:07.593 --> 00:04:10.220
and the harder I work, the luckier I get.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.649
I thought it was an amazing saying.

00:04:12.649 --> 00:04:15.069
Sometimes in life to be successful,

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:17.059
often times, most of the time,

00:04:17.059 --> 00:04:19.892
you have to follow your instincts.

00:04:20.918 --> 00:04:23.788
You have to follow your gut.

00:04:23.788 --> 00:04:24.861
You have to.

00:04:24.861 --> 00:04:26.757
Your parents may say wrong.

00:04:26.757 --> 00:04:29.756
Your whoever may say wrong.

00:04:29.756 --> 00:04:30.907
But you have to do it.

00:04:30.907 --> 00:04:35.074
So much of success is seeing
yourself as victorious.

00:04:36.171 --> 00:04:37.206
You have to see yourself,

00:04:37.206 --> 00:04:39.718
you have to really believe in yourself.

00:04:39.718 --> 00:04:41.078
And sometimes it's hard.

00:04:41.078 --> 00:04:43.154
You know, you've had failures,
you've had weaknesses,

00:04:43.154 --> 00:04:44.979
you've had other things.

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:49.048
You have to see yourself
as a one man band.

00:04:49.048 --> 00:04:50.515
Don't rely too much on other people

00:04:50.515 --> 00:04:52.070
because they'll let ya down.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:56.049
You have to see yourself as victorious.

00:04:56.049 --> 00:05:00.216
And to be a winner you have
to think like a winner.

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:03.256
If you don't think like a winner,

00:05:03.256 --> 00:05:05.108
it's just never going to happen.

00:05:05.108 --> 00:05:07.566
Many people have been
asking about my desk,

00:05:07.566 --> 00:05:10.095
and the fact that I have
so many papers on my desk.

00:05:10.095 --> 00:05:13.371
It's actually very neat, I
know where everything is.

00:05:13.371 --> 00:05:16.548
But if you look around, I
mean, there's a lot of stuff.

00:05:16.548 --> 00:05:18.510
The fact is, I'm very busy.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.260
I always noticed that busy people,

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.725
people that are very
successful in many cases,

00:05:23.725 --> 00:05:26.540
have a lot going on on their desk.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.738
I have papers from deals.

00:05:28.738 --> 00:05:31.652
I have papers from just about everything.

00:05:31.652 --> 00:05:33.642
I like to read up on things.

00:05:33.642 --> 00:05:35.989
I like to study what I'm doing.

00:05:35.989 --> 00:05:38.269
So therefore, you have
a lot of action going on

00:05:38.269 --> 00:05:39.392
in your office.

00:05:39.392 --> 00:05:40.667
Now, some of the writers,

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:42.388
some of the ones that aren't so smart,

00:05:42.388 --> 00:05:44.269
they talk about the desk negatively.

00:05:44.269 --> 00:05:45.752
They say oh isn't it terrible.

00:05:45.752 --> 00:05:48.083
Trump has so many papers on his desk.

00:05:48.083 --> 00:05:50.071
Let's analyze everything on the desk

00:05:50.071 --> 00:05:51.979
and they do their best and they fail.

00:05:51.979 --> 00:05:54.941
But the fact is, I've
noticed over the years,

00:05:54.941 --> 00:05:59.494
successful people have a
lot going on on their desk.

00:05:59.494 --> 00:06:01.886
My desk is in my office,

00:06:01.886 --> 00:06:04.776
my desk is a very important part of me,

00:06:04.776 --> 00:06:09.166
and frankly, it's a very
important part of my success.

00:06:09.166 --> 00:06:12.946
Everything I do comes
right through this desk.

00:06:12.946 --> 00:06:15.841
So, if it's not perfectly neat,

00:06:15.841 --> 00:06:17.445
if there's a lot of action,

00:06:17.445 --> 00:06:18.410
I think that's okay.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.577
I actually think that's a good thing.

00:06:21.577 --> 00:06:23.667
You have to love what you do.

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:25.016
And I'm not going to go over that long,

00:06:25.016 --> 00:06:26.610
but it solves a lot of problems,

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.650
because when you love what you do

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:29.964
when you really love what you do,

00:06:29.964 --> 00:06:31.332
you work harder.

00:06:31.332 --> 00:06:33.321
I mean I have friends that don't love

00:06:33.321 --> 00:06:35.130
and I tell this story,

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.463
a Wall Street type, big guy,

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:41.347
but I was richer than him and
I'm richer according to Forbes

00:06:41.347 --> 00:06:42.599
I am much richer than him,

00:06:42.599 --> 00:06:45.326
so I like that, you know

00:06:45.326 --> 00:06:46.327
I like that.

00:06:46.327 --> 00:06:48.629
But this big Wall Street
guy and he's a big man,

00:06:48.629 --> 00:06:49.709
he has a son,

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:51.518
and his son is a wonderful human being

00:06:51.518 --> 00:06:54.063
which is amazing because
the guy's a horrible

00:06:54.063 --> 00:06:55.396
horrible person.

00:06:56.701 --> 00:06:58.732
But he's one of the biggest
guys on Wall Street.

00:06:58.732 --> 00:07:00.884
So he wants his son Alex,

00:07:00.884 --> 00:07:02.558
I'm going to use the name
Alex, it's not his name,

00:07:02.558 --> 00:07:04.268
but I don't want to get
this guy in any more trouble

00:07:04.268 --> 00:07:06.011
with his father than he already is.

00:07:06.011 --> 00:07:07.786
Alex isn't the strongest character.

00:07:07.786 --> 00:07:10.557
The father is a brutal vicious killer.

00:07:10.557 --> 00:07:14.426
So the father, has a
big firm on Wall Street

00:07:14.426 --> 00:07:17.089
and he wants Alex to go
into the firm of course.

00:07:17.089 --> 00:07:19.024
You know, come on Alex,
get in there, get in there.

00:07:19.024 --> 00:07:21.543
And in the meantime, all
these kids are graduating

00:07:21.543 --> 00:07:23.166
from Wharton and Harvard and Stanford

00:07:23.166 --> 00:07:24.901
and they're just eating Alex's lunch.

00:07:24.901 --> 00:07:27.104
He doesn't have a chance,
he doesn't have a chance.

00:07:27.104 --> 00:07:28.372
And he doesn't like it.

00:07:28.372 --> 00:07:30.118
He's not liking what he's doing.

00:07:30.118 --> 00:07:31.180
You got to love what you do.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.061
He's not liking it.

00:07:33.061 --> 00:07:36.346
So one day, but he can't
leave because of his father,

00:07:36.346 --> 00:07:38.244
I actually told him, why don't you leave!

00:07:38.244 --> 00:07:39.341
Your father's brutal!

00:07:39.341 --> 00:07:41.295
The father's just killing
the guy telling him

00:07:41.295 --> 00:07:43.759
you don't have what it takes.

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:45.388
I'm sorry dad.

00:07:45.388 --> 00:07:49.126
Oh this is great, so I say
you got to leave the father.

00:07:49.126 --> 00:07:50.507
You got to leave him.

00:07:50.507 --> 00:07:51.904
He said I just can't Donald.

00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:53.252
So anyway, he's a spoiled guy.

00:07:53.252 --> 00:07:56.025
He's a member of a very good
golf club in Westchester,

00:07:56.025 --> 00:07:57.997
which is a rich place in New York.

00:07:57.997 --> 00:07:59.634
And he's on one of the committees,

00:07:59.634 --> 00:08:01.116
he's on the greens committee.

00:08:01.116 --> 00:08:03.081
And they put him in charge
of redoing the course

00:08:03.081 --> 00:08:05.050
and doing this big
renovation of the course.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.209
It's a very good club

00:08:07.209 --> 00:08:10.242
and he's there every morning
at five in the morning.

00:08:10.242 --> 00:08:12.211
He doesn't leave.

00:08:12.211 --> 00:08:14.878
He did such an unbelievable job.

00:08:14.878 --> 00:08:16.633
The contractors respected him,

00:08:16.633 --> 00:08:18.347
everybody respected him.

00:08:18.347 --> 00:08:21.160
So the wife comes up, he's
doing so great Donald,

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:22.733
he's so amazing.

00:08:22.733 --> 00:08:24.668
And they give him an honor.

00:08:24.668 --> 00:08:26.377
And this is a tough group of people

00:08:26.377 --> 00:08:27.323
that have this club.

00:08:27.323 --> 00:08:29.691
You know, they're club
members and they're difficult.

00:08:29.691 --> 00:08:31.166
They gave him an honor.

00:08:31.166 --> 00:08:35.658
The club came in under
budget, faster by two months.

00:08:35.658 --> 00:08:37.553
He did an unbelievable job

00:08:37.553 --> 00:08:39.696
and the quality of the work was ten times

00:08:39.696 --> 00:08:41.117
what they ever anticipated.

00:08:41.117 --> 00:08:42.368
So they gave him a medal.

00:08:42.368 --> 00:08:45.346
I said, you know, Alex, if you were to go

00:08:45.346 --> 00:08:47.885
and do this for a living,
you ought to be a builder.

00:08:47.885 --> 00:08:50.856
Whether it's houses, or
clubs, or golf courses,

00:08:50.856 --> 00:08:52.391
renovations or this.

00:08:52.391 --> 00:08:54.696
Oh my father would go
crazy, he'd never let me.

00:08:54.696 --> 00:08:57.799
So Alex went back to Wall
Street where he was miserable.

00:08:57.799 --> 00:08:59.808
And a year later, he quit.

00:08:59.808 --> 00:09:03.576
And he started doing renovations of houses

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:07.221
and clubs and different
things, and he's doing great.

00:09:07.221 --> 00:09:10.839
So, you've got to love what you do.

00:09:10.839 --> 00:09:11.918
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:11.918 --> 00:09:15.536
I made this video because
Anuj Deben asked me to.

00:09:15.536 --> 00:09:17.488
I hope I pronounced that properly.

00:09:17.488 --> 00:09:19.487
So if there's a video
that you want me to make

00:09:19.487 --> 00:09:20.537
on a famous entrepreneur,

00:09:20.537 --> 00:09:21.896
leave it in the comments below

00:09:21.896 --> 00:09:23.579
and I'll see what I can do.

00:09:23.579 --> 00:09:26.057
I'd also love to know what
you think of Donald Trump.

00:09:26.057 --> 00:09:27.736
Do you love him, do you hate him?

00:09:27.736 --> 00:09:29.823
Leave it below, I'm going
to join the discussion

00:09:29.823 --> 00:09:33.990
and I look forward to
